{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786542990,"nonce":"ghaU54DrPyYQluOrO5c+bnult8I=","target":"2e1f5c546f7cf86f017ddc7df0b248ed114c4fc07a48d9287fe227934df49180","message":"**What is wrong.** `notification_policies.reasons` is required to be a set, and exactly one of the three places CONTEXT.md §5b binds a rule to actually says so. The DTO `validate` tag carries `unique`; the domain constructor does not check it; the DDL `CHECK` does not check it. A duplicate reason is therefore storable by any writer that does not pass through `httpx.Bind` and the request DTO, and the contract declares that value impossible.\n\n**The demonstration.** CONTEXT.md `:238-239`: *\"A bound (length, count, enum, regex) lives in **three** places — DTO tag, domain constructor, DDL `CHECK` — and they must be **identical**.\"* Read out the three for uniqueness:\n\nLayer 1 has it. `internal/notification/api/dto.go:268` is `validate:\"required,min=1,max=32,unique\"` on `CreatePolicyRequest.Reasons`, and `:285` is `validate:\"omitempty,min=1,max=32,unique\"` on the patch. Layer 3 does not. `Policy.Validate` (`internal/notification/domain/policy.go:306-324`) is two checks and no more: a `switch` on `len(p.Reasons)` against `MaxPolicyReasons`, then a `for` loop calling `r.Valid()` on each element. Membership and count; nothing compares an element to any other element. Layer 6 does not either. `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:122-123` is `CONSTRAINT policies_reasons_ck CHECK (array_length(reasons, 1) BETWEEN 1 AND 32 AND array_position(reasons, NULL) IS NULL)` — a length and a NULL scan, with no `UNIQUE` and no cardinality comparison.\n\nThe write path confirms nothing else stands in. `internal/notification/api/validate.go:16-18` is the whole of layer 3 for a create: `validateDraft` returns `materialise(scope, d).Validate()`, and `materialise` (`:24-47`) copies `d.Reasons` onto the row verbatim. `validateMerged` (`:55-90`) does the same for a patch at `:69-71`. The repository then writes it through untouched — `internal/notification/repository/config.go:206-209` ranges over `in.Reasons` casting each to `string`, and `insertPolicySQL` (`:169-174`) hands the slice to the driver. `config.go:176-180` states the division of labour in writing: *\"The domain's own Validate has already run in the service layer; this method re-proves nothing except that the row is well formed enough to reach the driver. The CHECK constraints are the backstop, never the error message.\"* The backstop does not hold this rule.\n\nWorth stating plainly, because the ticket should not overclaim: **no in-tree caller can reach this today.** The HTTP create and patch bodies are the only writers of `reasons`, and both carry the `unique` tag. There is no seeder, no importer and no drill fixture that builds a `domain.PolicyDraft`. The defect is that the guarantee rests entirely on layer 1 while two layers that are supposed to repeat it are silent, which is the precise shape §5b exists to forbid.\n\n**Why it matters.** The contract publishes uniqueness as a property of the *response*, not only of the request: `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:8407-8414` gives `PolicyDTO.reasons` `uniqueItems: true`, and the generated frontend validator turns it into a runtime assertion — `web/src/api/generated/validators.ts:1407-1412`, `v.check((items) =\u003e new Set(items).size === items.length, \"must not contain duplicates\")`. `PolicyDTO.Reasons` (`internal/notification/api/dto.go:47`) is a plain `[]string` with no tag on the way out. So a duplicate that ever reached the column would be served back on `GET /policies` and rejected by oto's own layer-7 client validation — the server's response failing the client the server generated. That is a 500-class symptom produced by a row the database was happy to store, which is exactly the §5b table's row 6: *\"500 + alert (means 1–3 have a hole)\"*.\n\nThe functional blast radius is genuinely small and should not be inflated: `Policy.Handles` (`internal/notification/domain/policy.go:247-255`) is a membership test with an early return, so a duplicated reason routes nothing twice. The cost is not a double notification. It is that `reasons` is a set in the contract, a set in the DTO tag and a bag everywhere else, and the next writer of that column — a bulk import, a policy-template feature, a migration backfill — inherits a bag with no gate to tell it otherwise.\n\nThis was surfaced while lowering the three `reasons` ceilings to 18 (`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:8410`, `:10511`, `:10555`). That change is correct precisely *because* `uniqueItems: true` over an 18-value closed enum makes a 19th element unreachable on the wire — an argument that leans on uniqueness being real. It is real at layer 1 only, and the storage bound stays 32 for the same reason: the column can hold 32 entries because nothing stops it holding duplicates.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/domain/policy.go:306-324` (`Validate`, the missing check) and `:32-47` (`MaxPolicyReasons`, whose comment now records this gap); `internal/notification/api/dto.go:268,285` (the `unique` tags that are the only enforcement) and `:47` (the untagged response field); `internal/notification/api/validate.go:16-18,24-47,55-90` (layer 3 for create and patch); `internal/notification/repository/config.go:169-174,176-180,206-209` (the pass-through write); `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:122-123` (`policies_reasons_ck`); `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:8407-8414`, `:10508-10514`, `:10552-10558` (the three `uniqueItems: true` declarations); `web/src/api/generated/validators.ts:1407-1412` (layer 7); `CONTEXT.md:238-239` (the three-places rule).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** Uniqueness is written in the same three places the bound is, or the contract stops claiming it. Concretely: `Policy.Validate` gains a duplicate check emitting a `reasons` violation with a `duplicate` code alongside the existing `required`, `max_items` and `enum` codes, so a duplicate comes back as a field-level 422 rather than depending on the DTO tag; and `policies_reasons_ck` is tightened in a new migration to compare `array_length(reasons, 1)` against the cardinality of the distinct set, so the column cannot hold what the contract calls impossible. A domain test asserts the duplicate is refused, and a test asserts the DDL refuses it too. If instead the decision is that duplicates are tolerable in storage, then `uniqueItems: true` comes off all three schemas and the `unique` tags come off both DTOs, so no layer claims a guarantee no layer keeps — but the current split, where one of three layers holds a rule the response contract advertises, is not a state to leave.","files":null}]}